Notebook[{
Cell["\<\
MATHEMATICA PROGRAMMING
5 Feb 2003\
\>", "Title"],

Cell["Rule Based Programming", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Recall a definition for the factorial function:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[n_Integer]\  := \ Infinity\  /; \ n\  < \ 0\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[\(-1\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox["\[Infinity]",
      DirectedInfinity[ 1]]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[0]\  = \ 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[n_Integer]\  := \ n\ *\ fac[n - 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", when multiple rules are defined, the more specific rule is applied \
first, and general rules are tried after the more specific ones.  ?fac lists \
in the order of rules."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Definition for the greatest common divisor:  the greatest common divisor \
",
  StyleBox["d ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "is the largest number that divides both ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["b",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".   The definition below is based on a theorem in number theory which \
says, if for integer ",
  StyleBox["a,b,c",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " such that |",
  StyleBox["c",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "| < ",
  StyleBox["b",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and \n                      ",
  StyleBox["a = k b + c",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ",\nthen gcd[",
  StyleBox["a,b",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "] = gcd[",
  StyleBox["b,c",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "].  Doing this (division) recursively until remainder ",
  StyleBox["c",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " becomes zero. This is called Euclidean algorithm.            "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(gcd[a_, \ 0]\  := \ a\), "\n", 
    \(gcd[a_, \ b_] := \ gcd[b, \ Mod[a, b]]\ \)}], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(gcd[3, 9]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(3\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["What about gcd[...] with one, three or more arguments?", "Text"],

Cell["\<\

Define our own differentiation algorithm
\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
Let's agree that we do only partial differentiation, so that dif[expr, x] \
will be zero unless expr contains x explicitly.  To expression this fact, we \
can say:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[f_, \ x_]\  := \ 0\  /; \ FreeQ[f, x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[a, x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(0\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The differentiation operation is a linear operator.  We program in the \
linearity of differentiation:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[\ f_\  + \ g_, \ x_]\  := \ dif[f, x]\  + \ dif[g, x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[\ a_\ *\ g_, \ x_]\  := \ 
      a\ *\ dif[g, x]\  /; \ FreeQ[a, x]\)], "Input"],

Cell["We know the differential of polynomials (actually x^n).", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[x, x]\  := \ 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[x_^n_, \ x_]\  := \ 
      n*x^\((n - 1)\)\  /; \ \((FreeQ[n, x])\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
After these definitions, dif[ ] can work with any polynomials.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Rule-Based Programming: operator overloading", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
Suppose we like to use the notation  S \[Or] T to denote union of two sets S \
and T, and S \[And] T to mean the intersection of the two sets.  Since the \
two special symbol \[And] and \[Or] is used for logical operations on the \
truth values True and False, can we use them for set operation?   We'll use a \
list to represent a set.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a}\  \[And] \ {a, b, c}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a} && {a, b, c}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Since the operation is not defined, we get back the input.  Here is a \
definition that \"overload\" the operator \[And] and \[Or].\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Unprotect[And, \ Or]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(List[x___]\  \[And] \ List[y___]\  := \ 
      Intersection[List[x], List[y]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c}\  \[And] \ {a, e}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(List[x___]\  \[Or] \ List[y___]\  := \ 
      Union[List[x], \ List[y]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c}\  || \ {a, e, r}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c, e, r}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, \ b}\  && \ {}\  || \ {c, d}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({c, d}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Functional Programming Style",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
In functional programming style we apply functions to expression rather than \
through control of execution flow to achieve the computational result. [That \
is, in functional programing style, we apply functions that do not have side \
effect, and we do not use explicit loops.]\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[n_]\  := \ Times\  @@ \ Range[n]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
@@ is the Apply[ ] operator; Times @@ expr is short form for Apply[Times, \
expr]. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Range[4]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({1, 2, 3, 4}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Apply[Times, \ {1, 2, 3, 4}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(24\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Apply[Plus, \ {1, 2, 3, 4}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(10\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Observe that:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Times\  @@ \ \((a + b + c)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\ b\ c\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Map[f, expr] is another function of similar style.", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[f, \ h[a, b, c]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h[f[a], f[b], f[c]]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[Sin, \ {1, \ Pi/2, \ 2  Pi}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({Sin[1], 1, 0}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Concept of Pure Function in Mathematica", "Title"],

Cell["\<\
The first argument in Apply[ ], or Map[ ] requires just the name of the \
function, e.g., Sin, not Sin[x].  We call such objects pure functions.  You \
can think of a pure function as a mathematical operator, ready to act on an \
expression.   Here is a general way of defining a pure function.  Note that \
Function[x,x^2] is equivalent to #^2 &.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Function[x, \ x^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Function[x, x\^2]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[\ #^2\ \  &, \ {1, 2, 3}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({1, 4, 9}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
A standard way of writing a pure function without given the function a name \
is to use # (or #1, #2, etc ) as the argument of the function, construct \
arbitrary expression, and end the expression with &, such as  #*Sin[#] &.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us look at a program that finds zero of a function, ",
  StyleBox["f(x)=0",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", by applying FixedPoint[]; this is an example of functional programming \
style.  First the built-in function:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?FixedPoint\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \("FixedPoint[f, expr] starts with expr, then applies f repeatedly until \
the result no longer changes."\)], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We use Newton iteration method to find the solution to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`f(x)\)]],
  " = 0 numerically, which is based on a Taylor series expansion to first \
order:\n ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        RowBox[{"                  ", 
          RowBox[{\(f(x)\), " ", "=", " ", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{\(f(x\_0)\), Cell[""]}], "+", 
              " ", \(f' \((x\_0)\) \((x - x\_0)\)\), " ", "+", 
              " ", \(O(\((x - x\_0)\)\^2)\)}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
  "\n If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\(\(f(x)\  = \ 0\)\(,\)\)\)]],
  " then we can solve for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`x\)]],
  " as\n ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\(\(\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \
\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \)\(x\  = \ 
        x\_0\  - \ \(f(x\_0)\)\/\(f' \((x\_0)\)\)\)\)\)]],
  ",\n thus the solution is a fixed point of the function ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`g(x)\  = x\  - \ \(f(x)\)/f' \((x)\), \ 
      i . e . , \)]],
  " the zero of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`f\)]],
  " has the property ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`g(x)\  = \ \(\(x\)\(.\)\)\)]],
  "  FixedPoint[] function gets the solution by iterating ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        RowBox[{\(x\_\(n + 1\)\), "=", " ", 
          RowBox[{"g", Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
                \(TraditionalForm\`\((x\_n)\), \ 
                n\  \[Rule] \ \(\(\[Infinity]\)\(.\)\)\)]]]]}]}], 
        TraditionalForm]]],
  "\n"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(newtonzero[f_, \ x0_]\  := \ 
      Module[\ {x, \ fp = f'}, \ \[IndentingNewLine]x\  = \ 
          FixedPoint[\((# - f[#]/fp[#])\) &, \ 
            x0]; \[IndentingNewLine]x\[IndentingNewLine]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(newtonzero[\((Cos[#] - #)\) &, \ 0.0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(0.7390851332151607`\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(FindRoot[\ Cos[x] \[Equal] x, \ {x, \ 0}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({x \[Rule] 0.739085133385284`}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
We can give newtonzero another definition so that it accept normal function \
(not pure function) as input.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(newtonzero[expr_, \ x_Symbol, \ x0_Real]\  := 
      newtonzero[Function[x, expr], \ x0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(newtonzero[Cos[x]\  - \ x, \ x, \ 0.3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(0.7390851332151607`\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Here is the way usage message is entered for newtonzero, you get the message \
when enter ?newtonzero:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(newtonzero::usage\  = "\<newtonzero[f,x0] finds f[x]=0, with trial \
starting value x0.  f must be a pure function while x0 should be Real.  \
newtonzero[expr, x, x0] finds x such that expr=0, where expr is an expression \
in the variable x (a symbol), and x0 is the starting value (a Real). N.B. the \
iterations need not always converge.\>"\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "More single-line ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "definitions:\nVector calculus"
}], "Subtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Grad[f_, \ arg_]\  := \ \(D[f, #] &\)\  /@ \ arg\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Div[v_, \ arg_]\  := \ Inner[D, \ v, \ arg, \ Plus]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(JocobianMatrix[f_, \ v_]\  := \ Outer[D, \ f, \ v]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TripleProduct[a_, \ b_, \ c_]\  := \ Det[{a, b, c}]\)], "Input"]
},
FrontEndVersion->"4.1 for Microsoft Windows",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1024}, {0, 695}},
WindowSize->{1016, 668},
WindowMargins->{{0, Automatic}, {Automatic, 0}},
Magnification->3,
StyleDefinitions -> "Classroom.nb"
]

